;; HARNESS: does claimgraph beat grep on time, cost, and accuracy?
;;
;; The bar the project has to clear. Anything built here must beat an agent
;; simply grepping through hundreds or thousands of markdown files at read
;; time — and until that is measured rather than asserted, the answer is not
;; known.
;;
;; Companion to lazy-rewrite.edn (task :t11 consumes this result) and
;; issues.edn (:perf-regression-gate shares the corpus and CI wiring).
;;
;; Read with: (clojure.edn/read-string (slurp "grep-baseline-benchmark.edn"))

{:harness
 {:title "Grep baseline benchmark"
  :status :proposed
  :question
  "For each kind of question a coding agent actually asks about a project's
   accumulated knowledge, which of three strategies answers it best on
   latency, tokens, and correctness?"
  :arms
  {:grep "Agent greps the markdown corpus and reads what it finds. Cost must
          include the READ tokens, not just grep's wall clock — grep returning
          7 files in 22ms is not the whole price of the answer."
   :graph "claimgraph on-demand retrieval: search / facts / recall / history,
           or the equivalent MCP tools."
   :injected "The compiled view alone, with NO query issued. This arm exists
              because the view's advantage is that it arrives unbidden; it
              should be scored on what it answers for free, and on what it
              cannot answer at all."}}

 :findings-2026-08-08
 {:note
  "Preliminary, measured on this project during the session that motivated the
   harness. Not a substitute for the benchmark — one query, one store, no
   ground truth — but enough to establish that the question is live."
  :latency {:grep-ms 22
            :grep-scope "notes + book + docs, matched 7 files"
            :claim-search-ms 166
            :ratio "grep ~7.5x faster"}
  :accuracy
  "For `reproduce-first review`, claim search returns one of six paraphrases
   of a single lesson, flagged as conflicting with two of its own siblings, at
   retrieval-score 0.0103. Reading the note answers the question better,
   because the note states it once. The graph's disadvantage here is
   self-inflicted (see issues.edn :admission-dedup) and the benchmark should be
   re-run after dedup lands — a graph that answers with one clean fact is a
   different contender."
  :read-rate
  "7 of 749 facts have ever been retrieved on demand (~0.9%); the most-read
   fact was read twice; last retrieval 13 days before measurement. Subject to
   lazy-rewrite.edn :t1 confirming the log records every read path."
  :structural-advantage
  "The graph's reply carries t-valid, effective-confidence, epistemic class,
   source-type and conflict links. Grep cannot produce any of those at any
   latency. Whether that is worth 7.5x is exactly what the benchmark decides."}

 :design-notes
 ["QUESTION KINDS MATTER MORE THAN QUESTION COUNT. Grep should WIN point
   lookups of literal text, and a benchmark that hides that is dishonest. The
   live question is whether the graph wins the classes grep structurally
   cannot serve, by enough to justify its share of the eager spend."
  "The injected arm gets no query. That is the point of it, not a handicap —
   agents do not reliably grep for context they do not know exists, and the
   view's advantage is proactivity rather than retrieval. Score it on coverage
   of the question set with zero queries issued."
  "Cost must be token cost, not wall clock alone. The grep arm's tokens are
   dominated by reading matched files; the graph arm's by structured replies.
   A fast answer that costs 30k tokens of file reading is not cheap."
  "Corpus scale is a variable, not a constant. Grep degrades with corpus size
   and the graph mostly does not. Run at several scales (tens, hundreds,
   thousands of files) — the crossover point, if any, is the finding."]

 :tasks
 {:b1
  {:title "Build the question corpus, stratified by kind"
   :size :medium
   :description
   "Realistic questions an agent asks mid-task, grouped so each arm's strengths
    and weaknesses are visible rather than averaged away:

      :point-lookup  — what does X do / where is X configured
      :temporal      — when did this change, what was true in June, since when
      :provenance    — why was this decided, what superseded it, who asserted it
      :conflict      — what is disputed here, what contradicts what
      :synthesis     — what should I know before touching this subsystem
      :negative      — questions with NO good answer, to catch confident
                       fabrication in every arm

    The negative class is not optional. An arm that answers everything
    plausibly is worse than one that abstains, and only a labelled no-answer
    set exposes that."
   :acceptance
   "At least a few dozen questions with balanced coverage across the classes,
    drawn from real sessions rather than invented."}

  :b2
  {:title "Ground truth and a scoring rubric"
   :size :medium
   :depends-on [:b1]
   :description
   "Each question gets a reference answer and the evidence that supports it.
    Scoring needs to distinguish correct, incomplete, and confidently wrong —
    collapsing those loses the distinction the benchmark exists to make.

    Judging will need care: an LLM judge is the practical option at this scale,
    but it must be blind to which arm produced an answer, or the graph's
    structured output will win on presentation."
   :acceptance
   "A rubric a second person could apply and land near the same score, and a
    judge harness that never sees arm identity."}

  :b3
  {:title "Make the three arms runnable and comparable"
   :size :large
   :depends-on [:b1]
   :description
   "One entry point that answers a question set through each arm and records
    per-question latency, tokens in and out, and the answer text. The arms must
    face identical questions and identical corpora — the grep arm and the graph
    arm reading different snapshots would invalidate everything.

    The grep arm should be a genuine agent loop (grep, choose files, read,
    answer), not a bare grep, since that is the real alternative claimgraph
    competes with."
   :acceptance
   "A single command produces a complete per-arm, per-question record for a
    given corpus and question set, reproducibly."}

  :b4
  {:title "Report latency, tokens, cost, and accuracy by question kind"
   :size :small
   :depends-on [:b2 :b3]
   :description
   "Per-kind breakdown, never a single headline number. The expected and
    interesting shape is grep winning point lookups outright while losing
    temporal, provenance and conflict — and the size of that second gap is the
    decision input."
   :acceptance
   "A report that makes the per-kind crossover legible at a glance."}

  :b5
  {:title "State the decision thresholds BEFORE running it"
   :size :small
   :depends-on [:b1]
   :description
   "Write down in advance what result would cause retrieval to be kept,
    narrowed, or conceded. Deciding afterwards invites reading the numbers to
    favour the code that already exists.

    A defensible starting position: retrieval earns its keep if it wins the
    temporal, provenance and conflict classes decisively at acceptable cost,
    even while losing point lookups. If it does not win those, it is not clear
    what it is for, and lazy-rewrite.edn :t11 should take the concede branch."
   :acceptance
   "Thresholds recorded and dated before the first full run."}

  :b6
  {:title "Re-run after admission-time dedup lands"
   :size :small
   :depends-on [:b4]
   :description
   "Today's preliminary result is depressed by paraphrase pollution, which is a
    known and separately-tracked defect rather than a property of the
    architecture. The comparison is only fair against a graph whose answers are
    deduplicated, so the run that informs the decision must come after
    issues.edn :admission-dedup."
   :acceptance
   "Before-and-after numbers on the same question set, so the effect of dedup
    on retrieval quality is itself measured."}

  :b7
  {:title "Wire the harness into CI as a quality gate"
   :size :medium
   :depends-on [:b4]
   :description
   "Once thresholds exist, regressions in retrieval quality should fail the
    same way a performance regression does. Shares corpus and wiring with
    issues.edn :perf-regression-gate; extraction precision belongs in the same
    gate (issues.edn :extraction-quality-gate), since prompt and model changes
    move all three silently."
   :acceptance
   "A CI run that fails on a material regression in accuracy or cost per
    question kind."}}}
